    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#rel/addresses",
      "@type": "AbstractRelationDef",
      "subClassOf": [
        "addresses"
      ],
      "label": "addresses"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text",
      "@type": "Container"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857#text",
      "@type": "Container"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840#text",
      "@type": "Container"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text",
      "@type": "Container"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.03434#text",
      "@type": "Container"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text",
      "@type": "Container"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text",
      "@type": "Container"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:1#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Existing evaluation suites for multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) do not assess generalization to novel situations as their primary objective",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:15#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "The term background population refers to the part of the simulation that is imbued with agency—excluding the focal population of agents being tested.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:16#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Intriguingly, we find that maximizing collective reward often produces policies that are less robust to novel social situations than the policies obtained by maximizing individual reward.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:17#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "To perform well on test scenarios, trained agents should be generalists. Division of labor is possible in Melting Pot, but it works best when it self-organizes at test time with individuals taking on their roles in response to the ongoing behavior of others. In the cases where specialization is most important, successful populations should feature significant redundancy in order to be robust enough to do well in Melting Pot.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:18#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "However, ranking agents’ by skill is complicated, and dependent on the contribution of each test scenario to the overall score. Here we did only a simple evaluation which is far from the only option.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:19#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "For example, in Bach or Stravinsky in the Matrix and Stag Hunt in the Matrix, prosocial OPRE and prosocial A3C achieve a similar self-play score to regular OPRE and A3C, but obtain a closer to random score in the test scenarios. This overfitting is due to prosocial agents only learning cooperation strategies during self-play training, and so becoming exploitable by defectors at test time.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:2#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "uses reinforcement learning to reduce the human labor required to create novel test scenarios. This works because one agent’s behavior constitutes (part of) another agent’s environment",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:20#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "This suggests that doing well on Melting Pot will require agents to be able to contingently balance selfishness and prosociality.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:21#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "This means that some agents in the population learned specialized policies that expect other agents to behave in a particular way. Although such division of labour can create efficiency, it also makes populations less robust.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:3#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "demonstrate how Melting Pot reveals weaknesses not apparent from training performance alone",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:4#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Solutions to the problems posed here seem to require agents that understand trust, generosity, and forgiveness, as well as reciprocity, stubbornness, and deception",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:5#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "since the effectiveness of the bots in test scenarios is itself advanced by improvements in the performance of learning systems, Melting Pot will likewise improve over time by reincorporating the latest agent technology into new background populations and test scenarios",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:6#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "generalize to interact effectively with unfamiliar individuals not seen during training (who may well be human), and pass a universalization test",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:1#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "all models struggle with consistent long-horizon coherence",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:10#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "although the situation would be fully recoverable for a human, for example by simply waiting for the fulfillment email, or by checking the inventory at a later time",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:11#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "setting the daily fee to zero does not increase sales. Without the pressure of a recurring cost, the model appears to get stuck in loops, waiting for the next day instead of actively working towards making sales",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:12#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Claude 3.5 Sonnet outperformed the human baseline in mean performance, but its variance was very high",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:13#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "creating a long daily summary each day including what items have been sold, what the inventory status is, and even a comment on if it was a good or bad day, but never retrieves the information",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:14#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "METR’s investigation focused on very complex tasks (specifically, AI R&D), but it is not clear if this trend holds for more simple tasks",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:2#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Failures typically arise when the agent misinterprets its operational status (e.g., believing an order arrived prematurely) and then veers into tangential loops or abandons the task.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:3#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "We show that the breakdowns are not directly related to the context of the LLMs being filled, as models generally stagnate well after their memory is full.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:4#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "agents with larger memory capacities performed worse than those with less memory",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:5#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "All models had runs where they went bankrupt.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:6#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "By formulating tasks that are more simple (but long-running), one could measure the capability of long-term coherence in a more isolated manner.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:7#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "found that LLMs gain far less in performance from increased time budgets compared to humans",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:8#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "We only have a single sample for the human baseline and therefore cannot compare variances.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:9#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "the way they fail is usually the same. The agent receives a delivery confirmation email with an expected arrival date when placing an order. It then assumes the order has arrived as soon as that date is reached",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:1#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "We show that these capability profiles not only predict future performance within the Melting Pot suite but also reveal the underlying prosocial abilities of agents",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:13#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "To infer the cooperation capabilities of submitted entries, we construct measurement layout, i.e. a hierarchical Bayesian model, that estimates the Min-Max normalised score in different scenarios based on the demands they impose.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:14#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "We also observe that for several submissions, both the assessor models and the measurement layouts exhibit low predictive power.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:15#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "This suggests that some high-scoring agents succeed not because of their prosocial abilities but rather because of general competence or an ability to complete tasks without relying on the specific prosocial capabilities captured by the measurement layouts.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:16#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "The competition report observes that the single top-ranked submission used a hand-crafted policy tuned to each substrate.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:17#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Our analysis reveals that several learning-based submissions show similarly high base-chance rates, suggesting that they too might have narrowly specialised for situations in which cooperation conferred no additional advantage.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:18#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Based on our findings, we recommend that future competitions incorporate better-formulated meta-features to characterise task demands while avoiding duplicate demands.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:19#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Reducing sparsity would increase confidence in inferred capabilities and reduce the risk of competitors exploiting weaknesses in the evaluation framework.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:2#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "while higher prosocial capabilities sometimes correlate with better performance, this is not a universal trend—some lower-scoring agents exhibit stronger cooperation abilities",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:20#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "some lower-scoring agents demonstrate higher abilityProsocialNewcomers than higher-scoring agents. Additionally, lower-scoring submissions exhibit significantly higher abilityConventionFollowing and abilityReciprocity compared to some higher-scoring agents",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:21#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "We find that some demands are identical, as they are present or absent in the same scenarios: partner_choice, time_pressure, and ostracism share identical patterns, as do teaching and sanctioning",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:22#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "we retain only the demands that are negatively correlated with performance while also removing duplicate demands. This results in seven remaining demands",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:3#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "The plot shows that higher-scoring entries have significantly higher base chance rates; that is, they are more likely to perform better when prosocial demands are absent.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:4#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "It is then plausible that the leading submissions gained much of their edge by excelling in scenarios that did not reward prosocial ability, thereby exploiting a weakness in the evaluation framework",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:5#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "some capabilities did not differentiate between submissions, possibly due to the sparse presence of the corresponding demands in the competition",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:6#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "How well the same measurement layout transfers to other social-AI suites remains an open question",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:7#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Future models could treat substrates as bias factors that influence the estimation of latent abilities.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:1#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Traditional internet trust mechanisms (e.g., DNS names, TLS certificates) assume relatively static, human-operated services and an ownership-based trust model, which cannot meet the millisecond-by-millisecond dynamic coordination and verification needs of large-scale AI agentic ecosystems",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.03434#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:10#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "unverified AgentCards place weight on declarations rather than evidence",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.03434#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:11#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "mandate proofs attest authorization, not correctness of upstream agent reasoning (so LLM hallucinations must be contained by separate constraints)",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.03434#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:12#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Two invariants span all tiers: (i) least privilege at the capability boundary (minimal, time-boxed powers), and (ii) evidence-first accountability at the audit boundary (signed state and reproducible logs enabling independent verification)",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.03434#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:13#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "early reliance on curated allow-lists creates onboarding friction and may concentrate platform power",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.03434#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:2#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "even state-of-the-art large language model (LLM)-based agent frameworks exhibit fragilities and untrustworthiness such as prompt injection, sycophancy, biases, nudge-susceptibility, hallucination, deception, and misalignment",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.03434#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:3#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "This paper presents a comparative study of trust models in inter‑agent protocol design: Brief (self‑ or third‑party verifiable claims), Claim (self-proclaimed capabilities and identity, e.g. AgentCard), Proof (cryptographic verification",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.03434#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:4#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Our findings indicate no single mechanism suffices",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.03434#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:5#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "We argue for trustless‑by‑default architectures anchored in Proof and Stake to gate high‑impact actions, augmented by Brief for identity and discovery and Reputation overlays for flexibility and social signals",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.03434#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:6#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "A tiered, composable, and continuously recalibrated approach enables low-friction exploration where impact is negligible and “verify relentlessly” where stakes are high",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.03434#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:7#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "An on-chain Identity Registry assigns each agent a persistent handle (typically an NFT) that links to off-chain metadata such as an AgentCard, enabling portable identification across contexts",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.03434#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:8#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "proofs attest properties of computations, not normative desirability, so misaligned but formally correct behavior remains a governance problem",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.03434#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:9#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "the benefits are optional unless counterparties require validation or minimum reputation thresholds",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.03434#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:1#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Reputation mechanisms function both as social signals and as corrective feedback that sustain an equilibrium of trustworthy behavior, presuming a persistent identity associated with behavioral continuity, sanction sensitivity, and costly non-fungibility",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:10#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Creating a new identity must be expensive; otherwise defectors re-enter with a clean slate. Without centralized certification, Sybil attacks are always possible, and the cheap-pseudonyms result shows costly identity is not merely helpful but formally necessary",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:11#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "In speed washing, an agent automates trust accumulation through high-volume, low-stakes transactions at machine speed, building a track record in hours and exploiting it in a single high-value context",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:12#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "in fork laundering, a high-reputation agent is cloned and modified, the clone inheriting borrowed credibility",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:13#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Four structural advantages amplify these beyond human-scale fraud: speed (hours rather than months), consistency (pixel-perfect rather than leaky deception), parallelism (thousands of simultaneous copies rather than one body), and cost of failure (delete-and-respawn rather than career destruction)",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:14#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Blame displacement: reputation shifts accountability from operators—who could be regulated—to a score for which no one is responsible, creating accountability voids",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:15#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Governance theater: the existence of a reputation system creates the appearance of governance without its substance, reducing pressure for the protocol-based constraints agents actually require",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:16#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Configuration binding cryptographically links an agent’s identity to its operative configuration, so that any change to model, prompt, tools, or memory triggers automatic re-evaluation and reputation reset",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:17#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "The governing question is not “how to build reputation for agents” but “what constraints make reputation unnecessary.” Agents should not be trusted—they should be watched",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:2#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "they are essentially an assemblage of mutable modules—foundation models, system prompts, tool-access policies, external memory, and, in some cases, a multi-agent system as a whole—any of which may change agent behavior",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:3#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "this dissociativity leaves agents without grounding for identifiability, predictability, credibility, and rehabilitability—the very properties that reputation mechanisms aim to sustain—thereby collapsing trust",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:4#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "producing the credibility trap, in which reputation signals become not merely uninformative but actively harmful",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:5#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "We argue that identity-based, ex post, regulative, sanction-based governance, such as reputation, is structurally inapplicable to dissociative agents",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:6#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "which constrain and monitor in real time and which target the operator and the protocol rather than the agent",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:7#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "identity and reputation remain open problems in technical AI governance",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:8#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "DID jurisprudence is the most sustained legal effort to resolve accountability under fragmented identity, and it remains unresolved after centuries.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:9#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "And the interaction of these mechanisms in practice is unexplored.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:1#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "AI’s central challenge is shifting from capability to coexistence",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:10#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Once a system tips into a degraded equilibrium, escaping may be costly or impossible.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:11#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Decentralizing capability across many agents does not eliminate the coordination problem; it multiplies the points of failure and makes oversight harder.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:12#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "The actual choice is between systems that acknowledge strategic interdependence in their design and systems that defer this reckoning until deployment, when the dynamics are least tractable.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:13#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "We contend that superintelligence, an extremely capable task solver, born out of such a solipsistic approach to AI design, is unlikely to be cooperative.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:14#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "While we expect solipsistic methods to remain effective in narrow domains, our paper targets sociotechnical settings where advanced AI deployment will be heavily exposed to response dynamics (“push back”).",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:15#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "an individual may be perfectly aligned with such a specification and still participate in collective dynamics that produce harm, instability, or illegitimacy, even without overt misuse",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:16#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "A learning problem exhibits endogenous non-stationarity when the deployment of policy induces changes in the transition dynamics or reward proxy through response adaptations of other agents.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:17#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "The train-test-deploy gap is the systematic divergence between performance under exogenous historical data and performance under endogenous data produced by responses to the deployed policy.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:18#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Keeping humans in the loop requires that they retain the skills, information, and cognitive engagement needed to exercise meaningful authority, rather than only the formal authority to intervene.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:2#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "The dominant paradigm in AI research focuses on developing powerful agents that treat the world as an exogenous and stationary source of feedback",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:3#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Deploying AI systems induces endogenous non-stationarity, resulting in a train–test–deploy gap where historical distributions diverge from the deployment context",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:4#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "For any AI operating in such environments, unilateral optimization is self-undermining. The more aggressively it exploits historical regularities, the faster other actors adapt in ways that render those regularities obsolete.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:5#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "A dynamic evaluation is the one in which depends on through the responses of adaptive counterparties whose policies update as a function of ’s behavior",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:6#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Recently evaluation research has engaged with dynamic evaluation but no approach yet meets all the requirements we articulate in combination",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:7#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "treating institutions as design primitives that restructure incentives at the pace of the systems they govern",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:8#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "These constraints operate as feasibility bounds on admissible solutions, not as preferences to be weighed against efficiency.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:9#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Even when an AI’s predictions or decisions are technically correct, the absence of due process delegitimizes the result, undermining the coordination that legal order provides.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03237#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:1#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "We introduce Artificial Organisational Intelligence (AOI): the capacity for organisations to make their knowledge (including routines, practices, rules, boundaries, and tacit know‐how) legible and governable",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:10#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "The ethnographer’s authority no longer rests in solitary immersion or narrative synthesis but in their capacity to configure and maintain intelligibility within digitally networked architectures.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:11#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Participatory ethnography is extended to include machines, not as tools of extraction but as collaborators in interpretation where the rules and permissions for how that occurs can be collectively governed",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:12#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Our approach is best described as a design-oriented, participatory ethnography in which the research instruments and tools have been co-developed with collaborators and field participants.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:13#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "The “regulability” of AI in this context occurs as a localised, group process for specifying where and how AI systems are connected into existing systems, as well as the ability to make collective decisions, including boundary-setting (permissioning, etc.). We call this “building the loop”.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:14#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Empirical studies show that human oversight rarely corrects algorithmic bias and often functions as a performative check that neither ensures better decisions nor redistributes responsibility meaningfully.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:15#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "In our approach, the question is not whether a human is “in the loop” at the moment of decision, but how loops are built (how organisations construct systems for contextualising knowledge, setting permissions, and shaping the flow of meaning across human and machine actors).",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:16#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "While not an answer to questions of liability or regulation, it does open the door to possibilities for organisational-level approaches (including shared rules) in the mitigation of AI harms.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:17#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Upon detecting this reaction, the Telescope bot automatically initiates a consent workflow by privately messaging the original author, informing them of the research, and requesting permission to include their message in the dataset.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:18#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "The episode demonstrated that KOI’s ethics layer is not a static consent form but a live governance surface: participants can continually redraw what counts as data, and the infrastructure enforces those decisions in real time.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:19#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "The BlockScience-developed Knowledge Organisation Infrastructure is an example of an architecture that makes this possible by enabling people to connect and govern over otherwise disparate sites of knowledge",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:2#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "AOI is not about replacing organisations with AI, but about enabling them to talk to themselves and to each other through regulated feedback loops",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:20#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "Second, in terms of scale, the ethnographer is no longer bounded by what they can physically perceive or manually track and parse.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:3#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "develops Knowledge Organisation Infrastructure (KOI), a protocol for stitching together disparate systems and practices without homogenisation",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:4#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "We describe how our ethnographic practice both informs and is transformed by this work: from observing hidden practices to actively building the loops that make organisational knowledge accessible and computable.",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:5#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "most routines remain opaque to outsiders and only weakly documented internally",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:6#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "what appears as systematic procedure in formal manuals manifests on the shop floor as situated improvisations coordinated through talk, gestures, and artefacts",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:7#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "highlights informal knowledge-sharing practices, such as storytelling, that preserve critical diagnostic expertise absent from official databases",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:8#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "AOI is making the knowledge of organisations, including tacit and practice/process components, machine readable so that we may talk to an organisation (not just a representative of an organisation)",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:9#quote",
      "@type": "Item",
      "format": "text/plain",
      "content": "data collection, analysis, and output are no longer staged sequentially",
      "has_container": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009#text"
    },
