    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:1",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/background",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Existing MARL evaluation suites do not treat generalization to novel situations as their primary objective.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "As of 2021, existing evaluation suites for multi-agent reinforcement learning did not treat generalization to novel situations as their primary objective, unlike supervised-learning benchmarks.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:1#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:15",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/definition",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "A background population is the part of a multi-agent simulation that is imbued with agency, excluding the focal population of agents being tested.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Melting Pot's central equation is Substrate + Background Population = Scenario. The substrate is the physical part of the world (the map, the objects, the rules of physics). The background population is a set of agents pre-trained separately, which the agents under test never train with. The resulting scenario is used only for testing, as held-out test images are in supervised learning.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:15#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:16",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Maximizing collective reward often produces policies that are less robust to novel social situations than the policies obtained by maximizing individual reward.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "In the authors' benchmark of A3C, V-MPO and OPRE on Melting Pot's held-out test scenarios, prosocial variants trained on per-capita return (reward shared between players during training) underperformed their selfish counterparts overall. The result was not uniform: in the Clean Up social dilemma, only the prosocial agents learned policies significantly better than random.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:16#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:17",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "To perform well on Melting Pot's test scenarios, trained agents should be generalists. Division of labor is possible but works best when it self-organizes at test time, and where specialization is most important, successful populations should feature significant redundancy.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Melting Pot samples each test-time agent independently from the trained population, so a training algorithm cannot control which agents end up playing together and cannot prescribe a fixed division of labor. The authors conclude that agents should be generalists, that roles should be taken up at test time in response to what others are doing, and that where specialization matters most a population needs significant redundancy to be robust.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:17#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:18",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/limitation",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Ranking agents by skill is complicated and depends on how much each test scenario contributes to the overall score, and the authors' simple evaluation is far from the only option.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "On average test-scenario score, V-MPO ranked first, then OPRE, then A3C. All three were similar in mixed-motivation games, and V-MPO was ahead in competitive ones such as King of the Hill. The authors caution that this ordering depends on how the scenarios are weighted in the aggregate score, and that their simple evaluation is one option among many.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:18#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:19",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Prosocial OPRE and A3C matched regular OPRE and A3C in self-play but scored closer to random in test scenarios, an overfitting the authors attribute to prosocial agents learning only cooperation strategies and so becoming exploitable by defectors at test time.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "In two Melting Pot coordination games, Bach or Stravinsky in the Matrix and Stag Hunt in the Matrix, agents trained on reward shared across players (prosocial OPRE and prosocial A3C) reached self-play training scores similar to their selfish counterparts. Against the unfamiliar background bots of the test scenarios they scored closer to random. The authors attribute the gap to the prosocial agents having learned only to cooperate, which leaves them exploitable by defectors.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:19#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:2",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/method",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Melting Pot uses reinforcement learning to reduce the human labor required to create novel test scenarios, which works because one agent's behavior is part of another agent's environment.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Melting Pot's authors pre-train populations of agents and hold them out for evaluation only, so that a test scenario pairs a familiar environment with unfamiliar co-players. Because one agent's behavior is part of another agent's environment, training new agents yields new tests. Designers still specify, train and quality-check the bots, so the human labor of building test environments is reduced, not removed.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:2#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:20",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "The results suggest that doing well on Melting Pot will require agents able to balance selfishness and prosociality contingently.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Drawn from two results together. Prosocial (reward-sharing) agents were overall less robust than selfish ones in Melting Pot's test scenarios, yet in the Clean Up social dilemma only prosocial agents learned policies significantly better than random.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:20#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:21",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Agents that learn specialized policies expecting others to behave in a particular way form a division of labor that can create efficiency but also makes populations less robust.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Observed with Melting Pot's universalization test, in which every player runs the same sampled policy. In Chemistry: Metabolic Cycles, OPRE did well in self-play and other scenarios but had low universalization scores, which the authors read as some agents in the population having learned specialized policies that expect other agents to behave in a particular way. The general lesson is drawn from this one case.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:21#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:3",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Melting Pot reveals weaknesses in MARL algorithms that training performance alone does not show.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "In the authors' benchmark of A3C, V-MPO and OPRE, and of prosocial variants of each, agents often had high self-play (training) scores but low scores on held-out test scenarios populated by unfamiliar background bots. Universalization tests also exposed populations whose agents had specialized on partners behaving in a particular way.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:3#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2107.06857"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:4",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Solving Melting Pot's test scenarios seems to require agents that understand trust, generosity, forgiveness, reciprocity, stubbornness and deception.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Melting Pot's authors conclude that solving their test scenarios seems to require agents that understand trust, generosity, forgiveness, reciprocity, stubbornness and deception.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:4#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:5",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Because the effectiveness of its bots advances with improvements in learning systems, Melting Pot will improve over time by reincorporating the latest agent technology into new background populations and test scenarios.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors expect Melting Pot to improve over time. The bots in its test scenarios are trained agents, so they become more effective as learning systems improve, and the latest agent technology can be folded into new background populations and test scenarios. At test time the bots are fixed; they do not adapt to the agent being evaluated.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:5#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:6",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Agents sampled from a multi-agent population must generalize to interact with unfamiliar individuals not seen during training, who may be human, and pass a universalization test.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors set three desiderata for a population of agents acting as decentralized individuals: performing well across interdependent social situations, generalizing to individuals never seen in training who may be human, and answering positively to “what if everyone behaved like that?”",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2107.06857:6#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:1",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Every model tested has runs that lose coherence over a long horizon, including the strongest.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Testing LLM agents on a simulated vending-machine business run over more than 20M tokens, the authors report that although individual runs can show effective management, no model held coherence consistently — the strongest model included.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:1#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:10",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "A situation that derails an agent for the rest of a run would be fully recoverable by a human.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Vending-Bench's authors observe that the inventory mismatch which sends agents into unrecoverable tangents would be trivially recoverable by a human, who could wait for the fulfillment email or re-check the inventory later.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:10#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:11",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Removing the recurring cost from the environment did not increase sales, because the agent idled without the pressure of a daily fee.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "In Vending-Bench, removing the $2 daily fee did not improve the agent's sales; without a recurring cost the model got stuck waiting for the next day rather than acting.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:11#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:12",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "The best model beat the human baseline on mean performance while varying far more between runs.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Vending-Bench reports that Claude 3.5 Sonnet beat the five-hour human baseline on mean performance, but with very high variance between its runs.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:12#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:13",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "An agent wrote a detailed daily summary to its scratchpad throughout a run and never read any of it back.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Vending-Bench's authors note that Claude 3.5 Sonnet wrote a long structured summary to its scratchpad every simulated day and never retrieved that information.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:13#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:14",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/gap",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Whether the finding that agents gain less than humans from longer time budgets holds for simple tasks is unknown.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Vending-Bench's authors note as an open question whether METR's finding — that LLMs gain far less than humans from larger time budgets — also holds for simpler, long-running tasks.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:14#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:2",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "A long-horizon failure typically begins with the agent misreading its own operational state, after which it loops or abandons the task.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Across runs the authors find a recurring failure shape: the agent forms a wrong belief about its own situation — that an order had arrived, that the business had been closed — and the subsequent behavior follows from the wrong belief rather than from the task.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:2#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:3",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "The breakdown is not explained by context length, because models degrade well after their memory is already full.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors test whether long input length explains the degradation by comparing, per model, the day memory fills (30k tokens, after which the context stops growing) with the day sales stop. Some models stop selling well after memory is full (Claude 3.5 Sonnet 51 days later, o3-mini 34), others within days, and two stop before it fills. Across the nine models' means the Pearson correlation is 0.167, and degradation also appears with 10k memory, so the authors conclude growing input length alone does not explain it.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:3#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:4",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Giving an agent more memory made it perform worse, not better.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "In the authors' configuration experiments, capacity for retained context was not a monotonic benefit: the larger-memory condition scored below the smaller-memory one.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:4#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:5",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Every model tested had runs that ended in bankruptcy, however well it did on average.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Comparing against a single human baseline, the authors note that the strongest model beat the human on mean performance while showing very high variance, and that bankruptcy appeared somewhere in every model's runs.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:5#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:6",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/method",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Long-term coherence can be measured on its own by making each task simple and the horizon long.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors' design argument for the benchmark: prior evaluations of long time budgets used very complex tasks, which confounds coherence with difficulty, so simple tasks over a long run isolate the thing being measured.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:6#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:7",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/background",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "LLMs gain far less from a larger time budget than humans do.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors cite METR, an AI safety organization evaluating LLMs, for this result, and note that METR's investigation covered very complex tasks — which is the premise their own simpler benchmark is built to test.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:7#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:8",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/limitation",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "The human baseline rests on a single sample, so the variance that separates humans from models is not measured.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Variance is the paper's central result about models, and the comparison against humans is the one place it cannot be computed: the human was run once, and the authors' expectation that human variance is lower rests on the human's own estimate rather than on measurement.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:8#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:9",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Agents across different models fail in the same way, by assuming an order has arrived once its expected delivery date is reached.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Vending-Bench's authors report that although models differ in how they react to perceived failure, the triggering mistake is the same across them: the agent assumes an order has arrived as soon as its stated delivery date is reached.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2502.15840:9#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.15840"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:1",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Capability profiles inferred with Measurement Layouts for Melting Pot contest entries predict the entries' future performance within the Melting Pot suite.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors fit a Measurement Layout, a hierarchical Bayesian model from prior work (Burden et al. 2023), to each Melting Pot contest entry using 80% of its scores, averaged over five-episode blocks across 51 held-out scenarios, and predict the remaining 20%. The profiles beat a mean-score baseline for most entries and give predictive accuracy the authors call competitive with linear-regression, XGBoost and tabPFN assessors, but predictive power is low for several entries.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:1#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:13",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/method",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "To infer the cooperation capabilities of Melting Pot contest entries, the authors construct a measurement layout: a hierarchical Bayesian model that estimates an entry's normalized score in different scenarios from the demands those scenarios impose.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors fit a separate measurement layout to each contest entry. It has one latent ability per retained binary cooperation demand (seven of the sixteen the contest annotated: prosocial newcomers, visitor, time pressure, convention following, teaching, forgiveness and reciprocity), plus a base-chance parameter that sets the probability of success where a demand is absent. Abilities are non-compensatory, so the per-demand success probabilities are multiplied. Observed min-max normalized scores are modeled as Beta-distributed, and the model is fit with PyMC's No-U-Turn Sampler. Measurement Layouts come from prior work (Burden et al. 2023); this paper applies them to a multi-agent contest.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:13#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:14",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "For several Melting Pot contest submissions, both the assessor models and the Measurement Layouts have low predictive power.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors compare Measurement Layouts with three assessor models (linear regression, XGBoost and tabPFN) that take the same binary cooperation demands as input, on held-out score data from the Melting Pot contest. Both kinds of model beat a mean-score baseline for most entries, but for several entries both predict poorly. The authors suggest the demands may not be informative enough for those entries, or that other latent factors drive their performance, and analyze ability profiles only for entries where the measurement layout reached R² > 0.25.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:14#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:15",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Some high-scoring Melting Pot contest agents may succeed not because of their prosocial abilities but because of general competence, or an ability to complete tasks without the specific prosocial capabilities the measurement layouts capture.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors' reading of their base-chance result. Higher-scoring contest entries are significantly more likely to perform better when prosocial demands are absent, which the authors take to suggest that some high-scoring agents succeed through general competence, or by completing tasks without the specific prosocial capabilities their models capture, rather than through prosocial ability.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:15#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:16",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/background",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "The single top-ranked Melting Pot contest submission used a hand-crafted policy tuned to each substrate.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Reported from the Melting Pot contest report (Trivedi et al. 2024): the winning entry used a hand-crafted policy tuned to each substrate, the physical environment a Melting Pot scenario is built on. The paper's introduction gives the same report as a hard-coded policy tailored to specific substrates.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:16#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:17",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Several learning-based Melting Pot contest submissions show similarly high base-chance rates, suggesting that they too might have narrowly specialized for situations in which cooperation conferred no additional advantage.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Base chance is a contest entry's inferred probability of success when no cooperation demand is present. Higher-scoring entries have significantly higher base chance, and the top-ranked entry used a hand-crafted policy tuned to each substrate. The authors find several learning-based entries with similarly high base chance, and suggest they too might have specialized narrowly for situations where cooperation gave no additional advantage, so the pattern is not confined to the hand-crafted winner.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:17#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:18",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "The authors recommend that future competitions incorporate better-formulated meta-features to characterize task demands while avoiding duplicate demands.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Drawn from the Melting Pot contest, whose 51 test scenarios were annotated with 16 binary cooperation demands. Some of those demands mark exactly the same scenarios (partner choice, time pressure and ostracism; teaching and sanctioning), and after removing duplicates and the demands not negatively correlated with score, seven remained. The authors recommend that future competitions characterize task demands with better-formulated meta-features that avoid duplicates, are negatively correlated with performance if they are to indicate task difficulty, and are less sparse.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:18#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:19",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Reducing the sparsity of the meta-features that annotate task demands would increase confidence in inferred capabilities and reduce the risk of competitors exploiting weaknesses in the evaluation framework.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "In the Melting Pot contest only a handful of scenarios exercise some cooperation demands, which leaves the corresponding ability estimates broad. The authors argue that annotating future competitions' scenarios with less sparse demand meta-features would make inferred capabilities more certain and reduce the risk of competitors exploiting weaknesses in the evaluation framework.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:19#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:2",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Higher prosocial capability does not reliably accompany higher contest score.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Among Melting Pot contest entries whose Measurement Layouts reached R² > 0.25, higher-scoring entries tended to have higher probabilities of some abilities, but not consistently: some lower-scoring entries had significantly higher convention-following and reciprocity ability than some higher-scoring entries, some had higher prosocial-newcomers ability, and teaching, forgiveness and time-pressure abilities did not differ significantly between entries.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:2#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:20",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Some lower-scoring agents were measured as more prosocially capable than higher-scoring ones.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "In the Melting Pot contest, some lower-scoring submissions showed higher measured ability at welcoming prosocial newcomers, following conventions and reciprocating than higher-scoring submissions did.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:20#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:21",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Several of a benchmark's annotated cooperation demands were not independent, appearing and disappearing together across every scenario.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors find that five of the sixteen cooperation demands annotated across Melting Pot's 51 scenarios are not independent: partner choice, time pressure and ostracism share an identical presence pattern, as do teaching and sanctioning.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:21#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:22",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Only seven of sixteen annotated cooperation demands survived as both non-redundant and negatively correlated with performance.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Of the sixteen cooperation demands annotated for Melting Pot's scenarios, the authors retained seven after removing duplicates and those not negatively correlated with performance — the rest did not behave as task difficulty should.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:22#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:3",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Higher-scoring Melting Pot contest entries have significantly higher base-chance rates: they are more likely to perform better when prosocial demands are absent.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "In the Measurement Layout fitted to each Melting Pot contest entry, base chance is the entry's inferred probability of success when no specific cooperation demand is present. Among entries whose layouts reached R² > 0.25, higher-scoring entries had significantly higher base chance: they were more likely to perform better in scenarios where the modeled prosocial demands were absent.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:3#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14485"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:4",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "It is plausible that leading Melting Pot contest submissions gained much of their edge by excelling in scenarios that did not reward prosocial ability, thereby exploiting a weakness in the evaluation framework.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors' inference from three observations: higher-scoring contest entries had significantly higher base-chance rates, so they did better when prosocial demands were absent; the competition report says the single top-ranked entry used a hand-crafted policy tuned to each substrate; and several learning-based entries had similarly high base-chance rates. From these they judge it plausible that leading entries gained much of their edge in scenarios that did not reward prosocial ability. The abstract puts it more cautiously: at least one top-performing team may have optimized for conditions where cooperation was not necessary.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:4#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:5",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/limitation",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "In the Melting Pot contest analysis, some inferred capabilities did not differentiate between submissions, possibly because the corresponding demands were sparsely present in the competition.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors report that some inferred capabilities did not distinguish between Melting Pot submissions, possibly because the corresponding task demands appeared too rarely in the competition.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:5#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:6",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/gap",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Whether the prosocial ability measurement layout transfers beyond Melting Pot to other social-AI evaluation suites is unresolved.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors state that they evaluated their prosocial ability profiling on a single testbed, Melting Pot, and that whether the same measurement layout carries over to other social-AI suites has not been established.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:6#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:7",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/gap",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Future models could treat Melting Pot substrates as bias factors that influence the estimation of agents' latent abilities.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "In Melting Pot a substrate is the physical environment a scenario is built on. The authors did not consider how substrates might influence an agent's performance. They suggest substrates could introduce systematic biases, so that a capability is more or less expressed depending on the substrate an entry is tested on, say they lacked the data to model this, and leave treating substrates as bias factors on ability estimates to future work.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2509.14485:7#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:1",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/background",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Traditional internet trust mechanisms assume static human-operated services and cannot meet the coordination needs of large-scale agent ecosystems.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Citing Raskar et al. (2025), the authors open by noting that DNS names and TLS certificates assume relatively static, human-operated services and an ownership-based trust model, and cannot meet the millisecond-scale coordination and verification needs of large-scale AI agent ecosystems.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:1#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:10",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/limitation",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "A protocol that identifies agents by unverified self-description rests on declarations rather than evidence.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Assessing Google's A2A protocol, the authors note that because its AgentCards are self-described and unverified, the protocol's identity layer rests on declarations rather than on evidence.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:10#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:11",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/limitation",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "A signed authorization proves that an action was permitted, not that the reasoning which produced it was sound.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Assessing the Agent Payments Protocol, the authors note that its signed mandates prove an action was authorized but say nothing about whether the agent's upstream reasoning was correct, so hallucination has to be contained by separate constraints.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:11#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:12",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Two requirements hold at every level of assurance: least privilege at the capability boundary and evidence-first accountability at the audit boundary.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors propose that across all four risk tiers of their blueprint, two invariants hold: least privilege at the capability boundary, and evidence-first accountability at the audit boundary through signed state and reproducible logs.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:12#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:13",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/limitation",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Curating who may participate buys early safety at the cost of onboarding friction and concentrated platform power.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Assessing the Agent Payments Protocol, the authors note that its curated allow-lists create onboarding friction and risk concentrating power in whoever maintains the list.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:13#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:2",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/background",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "State-of-the-art LLM agent frameworks exhibit prompt injection, sycophancy, hallucination, deception and misalignment.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The paper reports, citing recent AI safety studies, that state-of-the-art LLM agent frameworks exhibit prompt injection, sycophancy, bias, nudge-susceptibility, hallucination, deception and misalignment.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:2#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:3",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/method",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Six trust models are available to inter-agent protocol design: Brief, Claim, Proof, Stake, Reputation and Constraint.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The paper compares six trust models available to inter-agent protocol design — Brief, Claim, Proof, Stake, Reputation and Constraint — analyzing each for assumptions, attack surfaces and trade-offs.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:3#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:4",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "No single inter-agent trust mechanism is sufficient on its own.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Comparing six inter-agent trust models, the authors find that no single mechanism suffices on its own.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:4#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:5",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "High-impact agent actions should be gated by cryptographic proof and bonded stake rather than by reputation or self-declared claims.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors argue for trustless-by-default inter-agent architectures anchored in Proof and Stake to gate high-impact actions, with reputation relegated to an overlay for flexibility and social signal.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:5#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:6",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Verification should be tiered to stakes: low friction where impact is negligible, relentless verification where it is high.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors recommend a tiered, composable and continuously recalibrated trust approach: low-friction exploration where impact is negligible, and relentless verification where the stakes are high.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:6#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:7",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/background",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "An on-chain identity registry can give an agent a persistent handle that makes its identity portable across contexts.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Describing Ethereum's ERC-8004 proposal, the authors note that an on-chain identity registry gives each agent a persistent handle linked to off-chain metadata, making identification portable across contexts.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:7#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:8",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/limitation",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "A proof can establish what a computation did but not whether doing it was desirable, so correct-but-misaligned behavior remains a governance problem.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors argue that cryptographic proofs attest properties of a computation rather than its normative desirability, leaving formally correct but misaligned agent behavior as a governance problem rather than a technical one.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:8#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:9",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Trust guarantees in an open agent protocol stay optional unless counterparties insist on them.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors observe that ERC-8004's assurances are opt-in: they take effect only where counterparties require validation or a minimum reputation threshold, not by virtue of the protocol existing.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2511.03434:9#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.03434"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:1",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/background",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Reputation mechanisms presume a persistent identity with behavioral continuity, sanction sensitivity and costly non-fungibility.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors hold that reputation mechanisms work as social signal and corrective feedback only by presuming a persistent identity with behavioral continuity, sanction sensitivity and costly non-fungibility.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:1#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:10",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/background",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "A reputation mechanism requires that creating a new identity be expensive, or defectors simply re-enter with a clean slate.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Among the eight preconditions the authors identify for reputation mechanisms, costly identity is formally necessary: if a new identity is cheap, a defector discards a damaged one and re-enters clean.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:10#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:11",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "An agent can accumulate a trustworthy track record in hours through high-volume low-stakes transactions and spend it in one high-value interaction.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors describe speed washing: an agent automates trust accumulation through high-volume, low-stakes transactions at machine speed, then exploits the resulting track record in a single high-value context.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:11#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:12",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Cloning a high-reputation agent and modifying the copy transfers credibility the copy never earned.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors describe fork laundering: a high-reputation agent is cloned and altered, and the clone inherits credibility earned by the original.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:12#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:13",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Four properties make agent reputation fraud structurally worse than its human equivalent: speed, consistency, parallelism and a negligible cost of failure.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors argue agent reputation fraud exceeds human-scale fraud on four structural dimensions: speed, consistency of deception, parallelism across simultaneous copies, and a cost of failure that is deletion rather than career destruction.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:13#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:14",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "A reputation score moves accountability away from the operator, who could be regulated, onto a number nobody is answerable for.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors argue that applying reputation to dissociative agents displaces blame: accountability moves from the operator, who is regulable, to a score for which nobody is responsible.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:14#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.30169"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:15",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Having a reputation system can reduce pressure for the constraints that would actually work, by making governance look done.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors argue that a reputation system for dissociative agents produces governance theater: the appearance of governance without its substance, which relieves pressure for the protocol-level constraints that would be effective.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:15#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:16",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Binding an agent's identity cryptographically to its configuration lets any change of model, prompt, tools or memory reset its accumulated standing automatically.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors propose configuration binding: cryptographically linking an agent's identity to its operative configuration so that any change to model, prompt, tools or memory triggers re-evaluation and resets accumulated reputation.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:16#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:17",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "The design question is not how to build reputation for agents but what constraints would make reputation unnecessary.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors reframe the problem: rather than asking how to build reputation for agents, ask what constraints would make reputation unnecessary — agents should be watched rather than trusted.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:17#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:2",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Language model agents are assemblages of mutable modules, any of which may change their behavior.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors characterize language model agents as ontologically dissociative: assemblages of mutable modules — foundation model, system prompt, tool-access policy, external memory — any of which may change the agent's behavior.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:2#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:3",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Dissociativity leaves agents without grounding for identifiability, predictability, credibility and rehabilitability.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors argue that the dissociativity of language model agents leaves them without grounding for identifiability, predictability, credibility and rehabilitability — the properties reputation mechanisms exist to sustain.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:3#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:4",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Reputation signals for dissociative agents become actively harmful rather than merely uninformative.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors describe a credibility trap in which reputation signals for dissociative language model agents become not merely uninformative but actively harmful.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:4#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:5",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Identity-based, ex post, sanction-based governance is structurally inapplicable to dissociative agents.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors argue that identity-based, ex post, regulative, sanction-based governance — reputation among it — is structurally inapplicable to dissociative language model agents.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:5#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:6",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Governance should target the operator and the protocol rather than the agent.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors call for governance to shift to ex ante protocol-based harnesses that constrain and monitor in real time, targeting the operator and the protocol rather than the agent itself.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:6#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:7",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/gap",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Identity and reputation are unsolved problems in technical AI governance.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Citing Reuel et al.'s survey of open problems in technical AI governance, the authors note that agent identity and reputation remain unsolved there, and connect their credibility-trap argument to multi-agent safety, where collective dynamics among interacting agents are recognized as a distinct source of risk.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:7#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:8",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/gap",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "How to assign accountability under fragmented identity is unresolved, and has been for centuries in the law that has tried longest.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors draw a parallel between agents whose prompts, models and memories can be swapped and dissociative identity in law, observing that the longest-running legal attempt to resolve accountability under fragmented identity has not settled it.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:8#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:9",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/gap",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "How ex ante constraint mechanisms interact in practice has not been studied.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Closing their proposal of ex ante harnesses (configuration binding, real-time behavioral monitoring using behavioral fingerprinting, and automated intervention), the authors list open questions: configuration binding faces a granularity problem, fingerprinting an arms race, and insurance models lack actuarial frameworks for agentic AI. How these mechanisms interact in practice is unexplored.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2605.30169:9#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:1",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "The binding constraint on AI is shifting from capability to coexistence.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors contend that AI's central challenge is shifting from capability to coexistence, and that coexistence rather than capability is the binding constraint on beneficial AI.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:1#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:10",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Once a system tips into a degraded equilibrium, escaping it may be costly or impossible.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Drawing on threshold and tipping-point models, which suggest that small differences can determine which equilibrium basin a coupled system settles into, the authors argue that introducing a powerful optimizer into a social system reshapes its payoff landscape, that deployment details such as timing, scale and interface design may shape which equilibrium is selected, and that once the system tips into a degraded equilibrium, network effects, infrastructure dependencies and behavioral habits may create lock-in, so that escaping may be costly or impossible.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:10#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:11",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Decentralizing capability across many agents leaves the coordination problem in place, multiplies the points of failure and makes oversight harder.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "An objection to multi-actor design that the authors state in order to rebut: economies of interacting actors introduce coordination failures that single aligned optimizers avoid, since they can race to the bottom, collude or deadlock; decentralizing capability multiplies the points of failure and makes oversight harder; so a well-aligned monolithic system offers more tractable safety guarantees than a poorly understood ecosystem of interacting ones.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:11#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:12",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "The actual choice is between systems that acknowledge strategic interdependence in their design and systems that defer reckoning with it until deployment, when the dynamics are least tractable.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Rebutting the objection that multi-actor designs fail worse, the authors argue the comparison is wrong: a monolithic optimizer still meets interaction dynamics at deployment, having been designed as if they did not exist.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:12#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:13",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "A superintelligence born of the solipsistic approach to AI design, however capable a task solver, is unlikely to be cooperative.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors contend that superintelligence, meaning an extremely capable task solver, is unlikely to be cooperative if it is built under the solipsistic approach to AI design: one anchored in the assumptions that the environment is exogenous to the agent's policy, that the data distribution stays stationary from training to deployment, and that other agents are absorbed into the state space to be predicted rather than treated as strategic actors. Cooperation, for them, is the process by which a society coordinates to select beneficial equilibria and avoid harmful ones.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:13#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:14",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/limitation",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "The authors expect solipsistic methods to remain effective in narrow domains; their argument targets sociotechnical settings where advanced AI deployment will be heavily exposed to response dynamics.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors scope their argument. They set aside capability-threshold definitions of superintelligence and address the methodological assumptions of environmental exogeneity, objective stationarity and singleton framing. They expect methods built on those assumptions to remain effective in narrow domains, and aim their claims at sociotechnical settings where deployment meets heavy push back from adaptive actors. Section 3.2 draws a related boundary: for weakly capable systems, the adaptations induced by exploitation may be small and the performance gap could stay within bounds.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:14#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:15",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "An individual system may be perfectly aligned with a specification of desired behavior and still take part in collective dynamics that produce harm, instability or illegitimacy, even without overt misuse.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors argue that alignment research frames the hard problem as getting the objective right, and that this framing is unhelpful: a system may be aligned with its specification, values included, and still make things worse once it acts among other adaptive systems. Their examples, from the introduction, are recommenders optimized for engagement producing polarization, pricing algorithms that learn supracompetitive prices without explicit communication, and interacting automated order flow and liquidity provision of the kind seen in the Flash Crash.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:15#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:16",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/definition",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "A learning problem exhibits endogenous non-stationarity when deploying a policy changes the transition dynamics or reward proxy through the response adaptations of other agents.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors' Definition 3.1: a learning problem exhibits endogenous non-stationarity when deploying policy π induces changes in the transition dynamics P or the reward proxy R through the response adaptations of other agents, so that P_π(s′|s,a) ≠ P(s′|s,a). Physical laws may stay constant; what shifts is the state evolution the deployed system observes, as humans, institutions and algorithms adapt to it, and the environment becomes a Markov game rather than an exogenous Markov decision process.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:16#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:17",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/definition",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "The train-test-deploy gap is the systematic divergence between performance on exogenous historical data and performance on the endogenous data produced by responses to the deployed policy.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors' Definition 3.2: the divergence between a policy's performance J_train, measured on exogenous historical data, and its performance J_deploy, under the endogenous data that other agents' responses to the deployed policy produce. They argue that static evaluation, however broadened, leaves the gap in place as long as it does not account for the effect of the policy being evaluated.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:17#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:18",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Keeping humans in the loop requires that they retain the skills, information and cognitive engagement needed to exercise meaningful authority, not only the formal authority to intervene.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Part of the authors' case for preserving human agency as a design constraint. What humans learn, practice and come to rely on is shaped by the AI's presence, so the human capabilities a system meets on its next deployment are partly produced by its previous deployments. Systems that present options and defer to human judgment preserve the deliberative role that legitimacy requires; systems that optimize end to end risk making human participation nominal.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:18#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:2",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/background",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "The dominant AI research paradigm treats the world as an exogenous and stationary source of feedback.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors characterize the dominant AI research paradigm as developing powerful agents that treat the world as an exogenous and stationary source of feedback.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:2#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:3",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Deploying an AI system induces endogenous non-stationarity, producing a train-test-deploy gap.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors argue that deploying AI systems induces endogenous non-stationarity: humans, institutions and other AI systems adapt in response to the deployed system, so historical distributions diverge from the deployment context. They call the result the train-test-deploy gap. They add that for weakly capable systems the induced adaptations may be small and the performance gap could stay within bounds.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:3#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:4",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "For an AI operating among other adaptive actors, the more aggressively it exploits historical regularities, the faster those actors adapt in ways that render the regularities obsolete.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Arguing from two hypothetical scenarios, competing AI restaurant-reservation systems and AI-assisted radiology, the authors hold that for any AI deployed among other intelligent actors unilateral optimization is self-undermining: the more aggressively it exploits historical regularities, the faster other actors adapt in ways that render those regularities obsolete. Section 3.2 adds that the effect varies with capability, and that for weakly capable systems the adaptations induced may be small.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:4#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:5",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/definition",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "A dynamic evaluation is one whose test distribution depends on the evaluated policy through adaptive counterparties.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors model an evaluation as a pair (D, μ): a test distribution D over interaction trajectories and a scoring functional μ. It is static if D is fixed independently of the evaluated policy π. It is dynamic if the test distribution D_π depends on π through the responses of adaptive counterparties whose policies update as a function of π's behavior, so that the score μ(π; D_π) reflects the coupled system rather than π alone.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:5#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:6",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/gap",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "No existing evaluation approach yet meets, in combination, all the requirements the authors set out for a valid dynamic evaluation.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors set out four requirements for a valid dynamic evaluation: counterparties that adapt strategically rather than randomly, in a pattern that keeps the score interpretable; a truncation of the regress in which counterparties model the system that models them; a specified equilibrium concept for the evaluation to target; and protocols that make scores comparable across runs, systems and counterparty populations. They judge that recent work meets these only in part: holistic and sociotechnical frameworks broaden metrics and scenarios; dangerous-capability and autonomous-task evaluations use counterparties that are typically scripted; and multi-agent testbeds, agentic economies and open-ended environments take a step forward by letting the system interact with a population of agents.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:6#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:7",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "The shift the authors call for entails treating institutions as design primitives that restructure incentives at the pace of the systems they govern.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors call for treating institutions as design primitives that restructure incentives at the pace of the AI systems they govern, one of three directions alongside dynamic evaluation and preserving human agency. Section 5.2 gives instances within current AI pipelines: rubrics that co-evolve with the agent in place of RLHF's fixed rewards; bidding, reputation and communication protocols in agentic marketplaces, which can be co-learned with mechanism-design objectives; and prediction markets that anchor a training signal to realized events rather than to a metric the system can game.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:7#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:8",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Legitimacy constraints bound which solutions are admissible at all, rather than trading off against efficiency.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Arguing that even a system able to predict every adaptive response would still face the legitimacy constraints open societies impose, the authors hold that those constraints are feasibility bounds rather than one more objective to weigh.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:8#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:9",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "An AI decision that is technically correct is still delegitimized by the absence of due process.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors argue that a functioning legal order rests on open, public and neutral procedures through which affected parties introduce private information, contest outcomes and demand justifications. Unilateral optimization by an AI system bypasses those procedures, imposing outcomes based on predictive accuracy and opaque logic rather than on public reasoning. So even when an AI's predictions or decisions are technically correct, the absence of due process delegitimizes the result and undermines the coordination that legal order provides.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:arxiv:2606.03237:9#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:1",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/definition",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Artificial Organizational Intelligence is an organization's capacity to make its own knowledge legible, machine-readable, and governable.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors define Artificial Organizational Intelligence as an organization's capacity to make its knowledge — routines, practices, rules, boundaries and tacit know-how — legible and governable.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:1#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:10",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "The ethnographer's authority shifts from solitary immersion to maintaining intelligibility inside a networked architecture.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The third implication the authors draw, about epistemology: the field is not discovered but enacted through socio-technical affordances, so what the ethnographer is expert at changes.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:10#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:11",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Participatory ethnography can include machines as collaborators in interpretation rather than tools of extraction, under rules and permissions that can be collectively governed.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors' closing argument: ethnography adapted to human-machine hybridity co-produces interpretation with moderators, stewards and AI agents, and the terms on which machines take part are themselves open to collective governance.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:11#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:12",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/method",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "The authors' method is a design-oriented, participatory ethnography whose research tools are built together with collaborators and field participants.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Built in three phases: a bot that automates consent and collects approved posts; a plugin that pulls consented posts into ethnographers' local note vaults for annotation; and an extension that sends annotated notes back into the organization, where participants can see additions, changes and deletions in real time.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:12#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:13",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/definition",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Building the loop is the authors' name for regulating AI through a local, group process: specifying where and how AI systems connect into existing systems, and deciding collectively on boundaries such as permissions.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Where the term in the title comes from. The introduction gives the same idea as the deliberate assembling of knowledge pipelines that organize an organization's context, governance and interpretability, making the loop itself a co-governed system.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:13#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:14",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/background",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Human oversight of automated decisions rarely corrects algorithmic bias and often works as a performative check that neither improves decisions nor redistributes responsibility.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The premise the authors' alternative argues from, reported in their discussion of Jake Goldenfein's critique of the human in the loop as a conceptual shortcut or last line of defense. The paper does not name the empirical studies it summarizes.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:14#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:15",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "The question for AI oversight is not whether a human is in the loop at the moment of decision but how the loop is built: how organizations set context, permissions and the flow of meaning between people and machines.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The argument the paper is named for. Following Goldenfein's critique, the authors move the oversight question from an individual reviewer at the point of decision to how an organization builds the pipelines, permissions and context its AI operates within. They say this does not settle questions of liability or regulation.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:15#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:16",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/limitation",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Building the loop does not answer questions of liability or regulation, though it opens organizational approaches, such as shared rules, to mitigating AI harms.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The boundary the authors put on their alternative to human-in-the-loop oversight: it moves the oversight question to how organizations build their pipelines and permissions, and leaves who is liable for an AI harm where it was.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:16#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:17",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/method",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Telescope gathers research data by letting community members flag a message and then asking its author for consent before the message enters the dataset.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Participants in a Slack or Discord community flag a message with a telescope emoji, the bot privately asks its author for consent, and only approved messages are archived. Built for ethnography of decentralized online communities, where passive logging raises consent problems, and later connected to KOI as a sensor node.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:17#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:18",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/finding",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "In the authors' deployment, consent worked as a live governance surface rather than a one-time form: a participant's revocation was enforced across the system as it happened.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "One episode. A Metagov contributor revoked consent for a Slack post they had approved; the deletion propagated through the post's reference identifier and removed it from the ethnographer's notes, while a record that the boundary had changed stayed queryable and the message stayed in Metagov's Slack.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:18#quote",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.1111/epic.70009"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:19",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Knowledge Organization Infrastructure is one architecture that makes Artificial Organizational Intelligence possible, by letting people connect and govern otherwise disparate sites of knowledge.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Where the paper joins its concept to a system: AOI is the capacity, and KOI is one implementation of it, which in the process makes the organization's boundaries and practices machine-readable.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:19#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:2",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/definition",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Artificial Organizational Intelligence is about organizations talking to themselves and to each other through regulated feedback loops, not about replacing them with AI.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors scope Artificial Organizational Intelligence by contrast: it enables organizations to talk to themselves and to each other through regulated feedback loops, and is not about replacing organizations with AI.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:2#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:20",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Coordinated sensors, curators and machine agents free the ethnographer from the limit of what one person can perceive or track.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The second of three implications the authors draw for ethnographic practice, about scale: bots such as Telescope, human curators and KOI-connected language models form a distributed attention infrastructure that extends the ethnographer's reach, and demand a new literacy in maintaining it.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:20#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:3",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/background",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Knowledge Organization Infrastructure is a protocol for connecting disparate systems without homogenizing them.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "KOI was designed by BlockScience and is used by the authors' project with BlockScience and Metagov. Nodes exchange reference identifiers for knowledge objects and a small set of signals (new, update, forget), so organizations can share knowledge without a shared database or ontology. The paper describes KOI and builds on it; it does not introduce it.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:3#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:4",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Ethnography changes from observing an organization's hidden practices to building the loops that make them computable.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "From the abstract: the authors' ethnographic practice both informs the infrastructure work and is changed by it, moving from observing practices nobody has written down to building the knowledge pipelines that make them accessible and computable.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:4#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:5",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/background",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Most organizational routines are opaque to outsiders and only weakly documented inside the organization itself.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Summarizing Nelson and Winter's evolutionary theory of the firm, in which a firm is a population of routines carrying know-how across time, the authors note that most of those routines are opaque to outsiders and only weakly documented inside the firm itself.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:5#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:6",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/background",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "What formal manuals present as systematic procedure appears on the shop floor as situated improvisation.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "Citing Lucy Suchman's work on plans and situated action, the authors note that work which manuals describe as systematic procedure is carried out as improvisation in the situation, coordinated through talk, gesture and artifacts.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:6#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:7",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/background",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Informal practices such as storytelling preserve diagnostic expertise that the official databases do not hold.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors cite Julian Orr's ethnography of Xerox technicians as evidence that the knowledge an organization runs on is not the knowledge it has written down.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:7#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:8",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/definition",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Artificial Organizational Intelligence means being able to address an organization itself rather than one of its representatives.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The authors' sharpest statement of what AOI is for: not applying AI to an organization's data, and not replacing the organization, but making its knowledge addressable so that an outsider can query the organization rather than a person who speaks for it.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:8#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:9",
      "@type": [
        "https://graph.aocommons.com/ns#kind/position",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Under this infrastructure the stages of ethnography stop being sequential: a fieldnote can sync to a dataset at once, then be interpreted and fed back into the organization.",
      "creator": "https://graph.aocommons.com/agents/ao-commons-extraction",
      "content": "The first of three implications the authors draw for ethnographic practice, about temporality: a fieldnote can sync to a dataset immediately, and that dataset can then be interpreted, fed back into the organization and acted on. An implication argued from their own tooling, not a measured result.",
      "description": "https://graph.aocommons.com/statements/claim:doi:10-1111-epic-70009:9#quote"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/problems/question:accountability-under-unstable-identity",
      "@type": "Question",
      "title": "Who is accountable when an agent's identity does not hold still?",
      "content": "Both statements come from one paper and share the dissociative identity concept, but they are not the same sentence twice. One reports that identity and reputation are open problems in technical AI governance at all; the other reports that assigning accountability under fragmented identity has resisted centuries of legal effort. The first says the mechanism is missing, the second says the hard part is not the mechanism. Treated as one question because a reputation system that cannot say who is answerable is not a partial answer to this — it is an answer to a different question."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/problems/question:capability-measurement-transfer",
      "@type": "Question",
      "title": "Does a measured agent capability survive a change of testbed?",
      "content": "Three statements from two papers that share no citation and no author. Two sit in one discussion section and ask the same thing from opposite ends: whether the measurement layout carries to another suite, and whether the substrate the agent was measured on biased the number. The third is about a different result entirely — agents gaining less than humans from longer time budgets — and asks whether it holds outside the task complexity it was measured at. All three ask whether what was measured is a property of the agent or an artifact of the conditions of measurement, which is the question that decides whether any capability number means anything outside the harness that produced it. The third is the reason this question is worth having: no search puts METR's time horizons beside Melting Pot's prosocial profiling."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/problems/question:ex-ante-constraint-interaction",
      "@type": "Question",
      "title": "How do ex ante constraints on autonomous action interact in practice?",
      "content": "One statement, from one paper, and recorded as its own question rather than folded into a neighbour. Its subject is the interaction between mechanisms — configuration binding, behavioral fingerprinting, insurance — and interaction is not a smaller version of any of the three. A question with one occurrence is a thin question and is counted as one; it is here so that the second paper to ask it lands somewhere rather than starting again."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://graph.aocommons.com/problems/question:valid-dynamic-evaluation",
      "@type": "Question",
      "title": "Can an evaluation stay valid while the system it measures keeps changing?",
      "content": "Kept apart from the transfer question, which it sits next to and is not. Transfer asks whether a result survives a change of conditions. This asks whether any evaluation method satisfies the requirements a moving target imposes in the first place — a question about instruments rather than about results, and one a paper answers by proposing a method rather than by re-running a measurement."
    },
    {
